<item rdf:about="https://it.slashdot.org/story/26/10/12/1748203/hackers-heat-french-supermarket-fridges-sparking-fires-and-spoiling-food?utm_source=rss1.0mainlinkanon&amp;utm_medium=feed">
<title>Hackers Heat French Supermarket Fridges, Sparking Fires and Spoiling Food</title>
<link>https://it.slashdot.org/story/26/10/12/1748203/hackers-heat-french-supermarket-fridges-sparking-fires-and-spoiling-food?utm_source=rss1.0mainlinkanon&amp;utm_medium=feed</link>
<description>Hackers remotely manipulated connected refrigeration systems at hundreds of French supermarkets, reportedly pushing some fridges and freezers to 50C (122F), spoiling food and even sparking fires. According to Reuters, French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu ordered a cybersecurity investigation into the hacks, with similar incidents reported in Spain, Portugal, Poland, Germany, and Belgium. In France, Carrefour reported 80 affected stores, Monoprix said 5% of its locations were hit, and Les Mousquetaires said refrigerators at roughly 150 Intermarche stores were heated to 50C for six hours. From the report: Supermarket cold aisles are typically run by control panels that manage an entire store's fridges and freezers and can be accessed over the internet, which lets maintenance companies adjust them from anywhere. The source close to Lecornu said ANSSI had identified a vulnerability in software supplied by Danish company Danfoss.
 
Danfoss said it was "aware of incidents in France affecting a limited number of refrigeration management systems." The affected units "were running outdated firmware," Danfoss said, adding that it had "long advised customers to keep supported systems updated with the latest firmware and to migrate legacy systems to current-generation solutions."
 
On August 9, cybersecurity firm Claroty's Team82 published research detailing vulnerabilities in the Danfoss AK-SM 800A controller. According to the report, software updates issued by Danfoss addressed issues that could have allowed attackers to bypass authentication controls and execute code on compromised devices.&lt;p&gt;&lt;div class="share_submission" style="position:relative;"&gt;
&lt;a class="slashpop" href="http://twitter.com/home?status=Hackers+Heat+French+Supermarket+Fridges%2C+Sparking+Fires+and+Spoiling+Food%3A+https%3A%2F%2Fit.slashdot.org%2Fstory%2F26%2F10%2F12%2F1748203%2F%3Futm_source%3Dtwitter%26utm_medium%3Dtwitter"&gt;&lt;img src="https://a.fsdn.com/sd/twitter_icon_large.png"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a class="slashpop" href="http://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fit.slashdot.org%2Fstory%2F26%2F10%2F12%2F1748203%2Fhackers-heat-french-supermarket-fridges-sparking-fires-and-spoiling-food%3Futm_source%3Dslashdot%26utm_medium%3Dfacebook"&gt;&lt;img src="https://a.fsdn.com/sd/facebook_icon_large.png"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;



&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://it.slashdot.org/story/26/10/12/1748203/hackers-heat-french-supermarket-fridges-sparking-fires-and-spoiling-food?utm_source=rss1.0moreanon&amp;amp;utm_medium=feed"&gt;Read more of this story&lt;/a&gt; at Slashdot.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;iframe src="https://slashdot.org/slashdot-it.pl?op=discuss&amp;amp;id=24122086&amp;amp;smallembed=1" style="height: 300px; width: 100%; border: none;"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</description>
